(c) change the 100.00 comparison to 99.99 — SURVIVES. FAIL.
  Mutation: web/templates/components/whatif/guardrail_optimizer.html:179 `{{if eq $cut "100.00"}}` → `{{if eq $cut "99.99"}}`
  cd <scratch>/gv3/mutc && go test -count=1 -v -run TestGuardrailOptimizerWorstAnnualCutSaturationExplained ./internal/handlers/whatif/
    --- PASS: TestGuardrailOptimizerWorstAnnualCutSaturationExplained (0.01s)
  cd <scratch>/gv3/mutc && go test -count=1 ./internal/handlers/whatif/
    ok  	budget2/internal/handlers/whatif	55.067s      ← ENTIRE package still green
  The mutation moves the explanation to the WRONG row. Probe on the mutated tree:
      CUT CELL: "100.00%"
      CUT CELL: "99.99% (no funded living spending for a full year in at least 5% of futures — usually portfolio depletion; see Portfolio depletion below)"
  WHY THE TEST MISSES IT — two independent defects in guardrail_plan_floor_test.go:
    1. `strings.Count(body, explanation) != 1` is invariant under moving the explanation
       from the 100.00 row to the 99.99 row: the count stays 1 either way.
    2. The second guard is vacuous by construction:
         if idx := strings.Index(body, "99.99%"); idx < 0 || strings.Contains(body[idx:idx+len("99.99%")+5], "no funded")
       the window is 6+5 = 11 bytes, i.e. exactly "99.99% (no ", which can never contain the
       9-byte needle "no funded" (it would have to start at offset 8 of an 11-byte window).
       This is the "guard one element away" failure mode: it is anchored 5 bytes short of
       where the artifact sits.
  REQUIRED: assert on the RENDERED cell strings — e.g. that the cell whose text starts
  "100.00%" contains the explanation AND the cell whose text starts "99.99%" does not
  (a widened/unbounded window, or a per-<td> extraction).

